lence.","confidence":0.6},"academicOrientation":{"score":28,"confidence":0.45},"profileConfidence":0.78,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Howard University","confidence":0.55},{"name":"South Side of Chicago","confidence":0.5},{"name":"Paris","confidence":0.4}]},"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"2d2508013739f33c4e3e45a02e5eadd88c04701e9abb06d2405b47318beeb9bc","reviewStatus":"flagged","validationWarnings":["author_included_for_review: Ta-Nehisi Coates"]},"primarySubject":"Memoir & Autobiography","secondarySubjects":["Race & Ethnicity","Society & Culture"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book is primarily a personal narrative addressing Coates's experiences as a Black man in America, while also deeply examining race and societal issues.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"fb9b606ed778bc1289720ae436b0c24f0b541bcac74491f883fd40ce325d05ef","summary":"In a profound work that pivots from the biggest questions about American history and ideals to the most intimate concerns of a father for his son, Ta-Nehisi Coates offers a powerful new framework for understanding our nation’s history and current crisis. Americans have built an empire on the idea of “race,” a falsehood that damages us all but falls most heavily on the bodies of black women and men—bodies exploited through slavery and segregation, and, today, threatened, locked up, and murdered out of all proportion. What is it like to inhabit a black body and find a way to live within it? And how can we all honestly reckon with this fraught history and free ourselves from its burden? Between the World and Me is Ta-Nehisi Coates’s attempt to answer these questions in a letter to his adolescent son. Coates shares with his son—and readers—the story of his awakening to the truth about his place in the world through a series of revelatory experiences, from Howard University to Civil War battlefields, from the South Side of Chicago to Paris, from his childhood home to the living rooms of mothers whose children’s lives were taken as American plunder.","displaySummary":"In a profound work that pivots from the biggest questions about American history and ideals to the most intimate concerns of a father for his son, Ta-Nehisi Coates offers a powerful new framework for understanding our nation’s history and current crisis. Americans have built an empire on the idea of “race,” a falsehood that damages us all but falls most heavily on the bodies of black women and men—bodies exploited through slavery and segregation, and, today, threatened, locked up, and murdered out of all proportion. What is it like to inhabit a black body and find a way to live within it? And how can we all honestly reckon with this fraught history and free ourselves from its burden? Between the World and Me is Ta-Nehisi Coates’s attempt to answer these questions in a letter to his adolescent son. Coates shares with his son—and readers—the story of his awakening to the truth about his place in the world through a series of revelatory experiences, from Howard University to Civil War battlefields, from the South Side of Chicago to Paris, from his childhood home to the living rooms of mothers whose children’s lives were taken as American plunder.","primaryTopic":"Black History & Culture","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Memoir & Autobiography","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Memoir & Autobiography","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Memoir & Autobiography","mappedSubject":"Memoir & Autobiography","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book is primarily a personal narrative addressing Coates's experiences as a Black man in America, while also deeply examining race and societal issues.","id":"subject-evidence-book-between-the-world-and-me-ta-nehisi-coates-1"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","rawLabel":"BIOGRAPHY & AUT